# SaphiraD 0.2.1 base system, systemd target (product: SaphiraD).
#
# Intentions ONLY. The package manager owns dependency closure; never
# enumerate resolved dependencies here (saphira-profiles(5)). Anything
# saphira-base already owns (bash, ca-certificates, coreutils, musl,
# accounts/shadow, baselayout, apk-tools, util-linux) is deliberately
# NOT repeated below.
#
# The kernel is pinned by LINE, not by version: saphira-kernel-7.2.3 is
# a per-kver co-installable identity, so naming it is a bare-package
# intention (recipe-rules.sh compliant) that still selects the known-good
# 7.2.3 line.
#
# ZFS is NOT in a clean image, for the same reason as the OpenRC twin:
# a base Saphira image carries no filesystem driver the distribution
# does not use. Root on ZFS is a deliberate flavour decision, built and
# shipped as such, not something a clean image acquires incidentally.
#
# Product identity is package-owned: saphirad-release ships
# /etc/os-release identifying SaphiraD Linux 0.2.1 (see the release
# package spec in the 0.2.1 plan). The OpenRC twin of this profile
# names saphira-release instead; the init choice is therefore visible
# inside the running system, not just in build data.
#
# Each entry earns its place:
#
#   saphira-base        libc/loader, shell (bash), core runtime, account
#                       and package tooling, CA trust, FHS/permissions.
#                       The foundation; never re-enumerated.
#   saphira-kernel-7.2.3
#                       the kernel and its modules, 7.2.3 line. Boot is
#                       impossible without it.
#   saphirad-release    product identity (SaphiraD Linux 0.2.1).
#   systemd             the init system for this target.
#   grub                the bootloader. Deliberately explicit: a bootable
#                       image carries its own bootloader, and later kernel
#                       upgrades need it.
#   e2fsprogs           fsck.ext4/mkfs.ext4 for the ext4 root filesystem.
#   openssh             remote administration, client and server.
#   iproute2            the `ip` tool (addresses, routes, links): named
#                       explicitly because no framework package pulls it
#                       in on systemd targets (see absent list).
#   nftables            firewalling.
#   acpid               installed for roles needing custom ACPI event
#                       handling, but never auto-enabled here:
#                       systemd-logind is the single power-event owner
#                       on SaphiraD targets.
#   vim                 the base editor.
#   iputils             ping and friends. A base host with no ping is not
#                       administrable; proposed explicitly, kept deliberately.
#   tzdata              a server with no timezone cannot reason about
#                       logs, certificates or scheduling.
#   intel-ucode         early CPU microcode (physical Intel hosts apply
#                       it before kernel init via /boot/intel-ucode.img;
#                       without it the CPU runs factory microcode).
#   linux-firmware-i915 Intel GPU blobs (GuC/HuC/DMC). Built-in i915
#                       initializes before the root mount; without these
#                       in the early boot image it wedges (proven on
#                       Alder Lake). ~30MB.
#   linux-firmware-intel
#                       Intel Wi-Fi/Bluetooth blobs.
#   linux-firmware-network
#                       NIC blobs (incl. common physical NICs). ~600MB:
#                       the price of unknown target hardware; trim for
#                       known NICs.
#   saphira-initramfs-tools
#                       the early-firmware generator (saphira-earlyfw).
#                       REQUIRED in-target: kernel/firmware hooks run
#                       chrooted here, so without this package no boot
#                       image is ever generated or refreshed.
#
# Deliberately absent, each a decision rather than an oversight:
#   - saphira-network + dhcpcd: the OpenRC network framework and its
#     DHCP client. systemd-networkd owns the NIC on systemd targets;
#     installing the framework alongside it is a conflicting network
#     stack, not a complement. Firstboot configures systemd targets
#     through networkd drop-ins directly, so nothing here needs the
#     framework's tooling.
#   - chrony (time sync is a service/role, not a base prerequisite);
#   - bind (running DNS services are a role; resolver support is musl
#     plus networkd-written resolv.conf, already covered);
#   - dosfstools (vfat is a UEFI/ESP need; the 0.2.1 BIOS seed carries
#     no FAT filesystem. UEFI profiles add it back);
#   - ca-certificates (already inside saphira-base);
#   - a graphical stack of any kind (see the headless policy);
#   - compilers, headers, *-dev, Perl, Python, PHP, databases,
#     QEMU/libvirt, saphira-tools, install/image tooling: builder-side
#     or role material, never base.
saphira-base
saphira-kernel-7.2.3
saphirad-release
systemd
grub
e2fsprogs
openssh
iproute2
nftables
acpid
vim
sudo
iputils
tzdata
intel-ucode
linux-firmware-i915
linux-firmware-intel
linux-firmware-network
saphira-initramfs-tools
